# 10. Performance and optimisation

We are targeting cinematic quality, but the game must remain smooth on the intended hardware.

| Area           | Approach                                        |
| -------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| Character mesh | Optimised game-ready topology                   |
| Textures       | Appropriate texture resolutions and compression |
| Hair           | Controlled strand/card complexity               |
| Shadows        | Selective shadow casting                        |
| Facial detail  | Preserve detail for close-ups                   |
| Animation      | Reuse skeleton and animation resources          |
| Memory         | Dispose of unused assets correctly              |
| Rendering      | Avoid unnecessary duplicate renderers           |
| Loading        | Load Prequel assets before the cinematic starts |

We should test on the M4 Max first, but also establish a reasonable performance target for ordinary desktop hardware.

# 11. Phase 1 production milestones

Here is the order I recommend. We should not build everything simultaneously.

1. Milestone 1: Character concept

   Design direction approved

   Establish face, hairstyle, body proportions, outfit, footwear and accessories.
2. Milestone 2: High-quality character model

   Build the full-body character, face, hair, clothing and materials.
3. Milestone 3: Rigging

   Build body skeleton, hand rig, facial rig and IK controls.
4. Milestone 4: Animation library

   Produce idle, walking, turning, phone interaction and look-back animations.
5. Milestone 5: Facial performance

   Implement eye movement, expressions, blinking and emotional reactions.
6. Milestone 6: Three.js integration

   Replace the existing primitive character while preserving the Prequel's story orchestration.
7. Milestone 7: Cinematic testing

   Test every important shot under streetlights, moonlight and car headlights.
8. Milestone 8: Final polish

   Fix foot sliding, clipping, animation pops, unnatural hand positions and lighting issues.

## 12. Phase 1 acceptance criteria

Phase 1 is not complete just because the GLB loads successfully.

The character must pass these tests:

- Looks convincing in a full-body shot.
- Looks convincing in a close-up.
- Hair and clothing do not look artificial.
- Walking has natural weight transfer.
- Feet do not slide noticeably.
- Hands correctly grip and hold the phone.
- Eyes track the phone naturally.
- Facial expressions are subtle and believable.
- Walking-to-stopping transitions are smooth.
- Turning and looking back feel natural.
- Car headlights illuminate the character correctly.
- Character performance stays consistent across camera angles.
- Performance remains stable during the scene.

